  "report_markdown": "DRAFT\n\n**Complaint/History**\n- Patient reports a 4-day history of intensely itchy, sore, and red skin.\n- Affected areas: chest, hands, arms, and inside the elbows.\n- Symptoms are severe enough to disrupt sleep and concentration at work.\n- No fever, cough, breathing difficulties, urinary symptoms, or gastrointestinal issues reported.\n- Skin is dry and cracked in some areas, but no bleeding or discharge noted.\n- Symptoms differ from prior eczema episodes, which were limited to the arms.\n- Over-the-counter steroid cream and antihistamines tried recently with no improvement.\n- No clear trigger identified; patient denies changes in shower gel, clothing, or known exposures.\n- Symptoms interfere with daily activities, including outdoor plans like hiking.\n\n**Relevant Background/Medications/Allergies**\n- Past medical history: eczema (previously managed with topical cream and shower product), past asthma (resolved).\n- No current regular medications.\n- No known allergies.\n- Lives with flatmates; works as a project manager at a pharmaceutical company.\n- Non-smoker; consumes a few beers per week.\n\n**Findings**\n- Skin: red, dry, cracked, intensely itchy; no pus or blood discharge.\n- Distribution: chest, hands, arms, inside elbows; not on face.\n- No systemic symptoms.\n\n**Clinician's Assessment**\n- Possible flare-up of eczema, though more widespread and severe than previous episodes.\n- Differential not explicitly discussed, but presentation is consistent with eczematous dermatitis.\n\n**Recorded Plan/Follow-up**\n- Prescribe stronger topical steroid.\n- Prescribe emollients for use in bath/shower, to be used regularly for 7–10 days.\n- Prescribe Fexofenadine (stronger antihistamine).\n- Advised to monitor for potential triggers (e.g., soaps, shower gels, fabrics).\n- Follow-up advised in one week if symptoms do not improve.\n- Patient has no questions and agrees with plan.",
